--TEST--
Every Core\Arr callback is handed its key as a `string` over a list, where the key is a position, and every one of them refuses an `int $k` — `rule:types/arrays`, `rule:types/conversion` and `rule:types/arrays`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The depth claim is *agreement about a type*. `rule:types/arrays` makes the `$key` a
// callback receives a `string` in every member and over every array shape, and
// a list is the one shape where that costs something: `SlotKey::Index` holds
// the position, so the decimal is rendered for the call and for nothing else
// (`crates/nvs-stdlib/src/arr.rs`'s module doc owns the mechanism). A member
// that handed the position on unrendered would still answer plausibly on its
// own line — the keys would read the same — so what is asserted here is both
// sides of the rule at once: every member accepts `string $k` over a list and
// reports the same keys, and every member refuses `int $k`. Only a `string`
// passes both halves; the neighbouring
// `arr-every-callback-is-offered-value-and-key` asks the arity question, over
// a map, and never asks what a wrong declaration does.

// A packed list: every key below is a position that was never stored as one.
array<int> $list = [10, 20, 30, 40];
array<int> $partner = ["p" => 10, "q" => 60];
array<int> $twin = [0, 0, 0, 0];

// Each entry is one member's report of the keys it offered, recovered from
// whatever that member does with what the callback returned.
array<string> $seen = [
    Core\Str::join(Core\Arr::keys(Core\Arr::filter($list,
        fn(int $v, string $k) => $k == "0" || $k == "1" || $k == "2" || $k == "3")), ","),
    Core\Str::join(Core\Arr::values(Core\Arr::map($list, fn(int $v, string $k) => $k)), ","),
    Core\Str::join(Core\Arr::keys(Core\Arr::mapKeys($list, fn(int $v, string $k) => $k)), ","),
    Core\Str::join(Core\Arr::keys(Core\Arr::groupBy($list, fn(int $v, string $k) => $k)), ","),
    Core\Str::join(Core\Arr::keys(Core\Arr::countBy($list, {by: fn(int $v, string $k) => $k})), ","),
    // The fold has no answer of its own to read the keys back out of, so it
    // builds one: a leading separator, dropped by the slice.
    Core\Str::slice(Core\Arr::reduce($list,
        fn(string $c, int $v, string $k) => $c . "," . $k, ""), 1),
    Core\Str::join(Core\Arr::keys(Core\Arr::unique($list, {by: fn(int $v, string $k) => $k})), ","),
    Core\Str::join(Core\Arr::keys(Core\Arr::diff($list, $partner, {by: fn(int $v, string $k) => $k})), ","),
    Core\Str::join(Core\Arr::keys(Core\Arr::intersect($list, $twin, {by: fn(int $v, string $k) => $k})), ","),
];

// Counted against the literal the keys of a four-element list have to be, not
// against each other, so nine members agreeing on the wrong string still fails.
var $same = 0;
foreach ($seen as string $report) {
    if ($report == "0,1,2,3") {
        $same = $same + 1;
    }
}
echo $seen["0"], "\n";
echo $same, " of ", Core\Arr::count($seen), "\n";

// The four members that answer about one entry rather than a list have no
// answer to read keys out of: they are asked for the entry whose key is "2",
// which is a string comparison against a position.
echo Core\Arr::find($list, fn(int $v, string $k) => $k == "2") ?? -1, "|",
     Core\Arr::findKey($list, fn(int $v, string $k) => $k == "2") ?? "none", "|",
     Core\Arr::any($list, fn(int $v, string $k) => $k == "2") as string, "|",
     Core\Arr::all($list, fn(int $v, string $k) => $k == "2") as string, "\n";

// `sort` reorders, so its report is its own answer's order — sorting a list
// *by* its rendered key is what leaves the order alone.
echo Core\Str::join(Core\Arr::keys(Core\Arr::sort($list,
    {by: fn(int $v, string $k) => $k, preserveKeys: true})), ","), "\n";

// The other half, and the two places it is answered from. Nine of the members
// swept above declare their callback's signature
// (`rule:types/callable-signature`), so `callable(T, string)` places the `$k`
// an `int $k` would have to satisfy and the mismatch is refused where it is
// written — none of them can reach a `try` at all, which is the whole point of
// the goal that typed them.
//
// What is left bare is an **option bag's** callback, and there the same
// mistake is still the runtime's to catch: `nvs_runtime::call_callable` checks
// each argument against the tags the closure declared, so a callback writing
// `int $k` throws `LogicError` — `rule:types/conversion` has no implicit
// `string` → `int` conversion, and the only widening it does admit runs the
// other way. Both counters are incremented from inside the same five blocks,
// so a bag that quietly stopped offering a key raises `asked` without raising
// `refused`.
var $asked = 0;
var $refused = 0;
try { $asked = $asked + 1; Core\Arr::countBy($list, {by: fn(int $v, int $k) => $v}); }
catch (LogicError $e) { $refused = $refused + 1; }
try { $asked = $asked + 1; Core\Arr::unique($list, {by: fn(int $v, int $k) => $v}); }
catch (LogicError $e) { $refused = $refused + 1; }
try { $asked = $asked + 1; Core\Arr::sort($list, {by: fn(int $v, int $k) => $v}); }
catch (LogicError $e) { $refused = $refused + 1; }
try { $asked = $asked + 1; Core\Arr::diff($list, $partner, {by: fn(int $v, int $k) => $v}); }
catch (LogicError $e) { $refused = $refused + 1; }
try { $asked = $asked + 1; Core\Arr::intersect($list, $twin, {by: fn(int $v, int $k) => $v}); }
catch (LogicError $e) { $refused = $refused + 1; }
echo $refused, " of ", $asked, " refused\n";
--EXPECT--
0,1,2,3
9 of 9
30|2|1|
0,1,2,3
5 of 5 refused
